<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Get Cool in the Shade. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Get Cool in the Shade.</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe preferences and experiences. Interactive: Express likes and dislikes in group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe preferences, using simple language (e.g., "I prefer saving energy wherever possible because it is beneficial and better for the environment."). Can express experiences during group discussions (e.g., "Saving energy is the best choice for the planet."). Can ask others about their likes and dislikes (e.g., "What do you think is the easiest way to save energy at home?" "Which do you prefer: switching off lights when not in use or using natural light?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 40%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary and grammar for the lesson's speaking focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and shows a picture of a sunny day versus a shady spot under a tree. "Look at these pictures! Which place do you prefer to be on a hot day? Why?" Students briefly answer, e.g., "I prefer the shade because it's cool." The teacher introduces the theme "Let's Get Cool in the Shade" and connects it to saving energy.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models phrases for expressing preferences and giving suggestions related to conservation. "I prefer using natural light because it saves energy." "Turn off the lights when you leave a room." The teacher emphasizes the /sh/ sound in "shade" and "switch" and the /t/ sound in "turn off" and "water," showing how to articulate them clearly. The teacher also introduces simple comparatives: "Using natural light is <b>easier than</b> using electricity all day."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'Turn off the faucet!', do I want more water or no water?" (Expected answer: "No water.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I prefer something, do I like it a lot or a little?" (Expected answer: "A lot.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue between two friends, Leo and Maya, discussing ways to save energy at home during "morning, noon, and night."</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Dialogue Excerpt:</b></li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Leo:</b> "Good morning, Maya! What do you do to save energy in the morning?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Maya:</b> "Hi, Leo! I always use natural <b>light</b> in the <b>morning</b>. It's <b>easier</b> than turning on many <b>bulbs</b>. I also make sure to <b>turn off</b> the <b>faucet</b> when brushing my teeth."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Leo:</b> "That's great! At <b>noon</b>, I prefer to find <b>shade</b> outside. It's cooler and saves <b>electricity</b> from the air conditioner. What about <b>night</b>?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Maya:</b> "At night, I prefer to <b>switch off</b> all unused appliances. <b>Saving energy</b> is <b>the most important</b> thing for our <b>environment</b>."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The dialogue provides examples of expressing preferences ("I prefer..."), giving suggested actions ("Turn off...", "Use natural light..."), and comparing actions using comparatives ("easier than") and superlatives ("the most important").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do Leo and Maya talk about?" (Gist)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"True or False: Maya uses many lights in the morning." (T/F)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the most important thing for Maya at night?" (Specific information)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking through controlled and semi-controlled practice of target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using imperatives ("Turn off...", "Let's save..."), comparatives ("easier than", "safer"), superlatives ("the most important"), and vocabulary (shade, light, water, energy, environment, turn off, save, switch) to express preferences and suggest actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion & Choral Repetition):</b> The teacher provides sentence frames on the board or flashcards, and students complete them orally using target vocabulary/grammar. E.g., "_____ off the lights!" (Turn). "Using <b>solar power</b> is _____ _____ traditional <b>electricity</b>." (easier than). Students repeat sentences chorally and individually, focusing on /sh/ and /t/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Preference Swap):</b> Students work in pairs. Teacher gives sentence starters: "I prefer _____ because it is _____." or "Which do you prefer: _____ or _____?" Students take turns completing the sentences with vocabulary related to conservation. E.g., "I prefer to use natural <b>light</b> because it is better for the <b>environment</b>." "Which do you prefer: switching off lights or using natural light?" "I prefer switching off lights because it is <b>safer</b>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Conservation Recommendations):</b> In pairs, students discuss two ways they can <b>save energy</b> at home during the <b>morning</b>, <b>noon</b>, and <b>night</b>. They must use at least one imperative and one comparative/superlative. E.g., "Let's <b>conserve water</b>. Turning off the <b>faucet</b> is <b>easier than</b> taking very short showers." "I think <b>reducing waste</b> is <b>the most important</b>."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Our Daily Conservation Chat" – Small group discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students form groups of 3-4. Each group receives a set of picture cards representing different conservation actions (e.g., "turn off lights", "use natural light", "recycle", "turn off faucet", "use shade outdoors"). One student picks a card and asks, "What do you do to conserve [energy/water] during the [morning/noon/night]?" or "Which do you prefer: [action 1] or [action 2]?" Other group members respond by expressing their preferences and experiences, giving suggestions, and using comparatives/superlatives. They must use target vocabulary like <b>shade, light, water, energy, environment, turn off, save, recycle, cool, easy, brighter, safer, most important</b>. For example, "I prefer to <b>recycle</b> plastic bottles in the <b>morning</b>. It's <b>easier than</b> finding a trash can sometimes." "Using natural <b>light</b> is <b>the most important</b> for me during the day." The teacher circulates, offering support and noting fluency.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The activity directly relates to "Conservation Morning, Noon, and Night!" by having students discuss actions at different times of the day, and "Let's Get Cool in the Shade" by discussing preferences for keeping cool sustainably.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to check understanding and application of language. Includes student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the "Our Daily Conservation Chat."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the small group discussion, demonstrating their ability to express preferences, give suggestions, and use comparatives/superlatives in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /sh/ (shade, switch) and /t/ (turn off, water).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., shade, light, energy, environment, turn off, save).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses imperatives to give suggestions (e.g., "Turn off the lights.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses comparatives (e.g., "easier than") and/or superlatives (e.g., "the most important") correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Expresses preferences clearly (e.g., "I prefer saving energy...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates and interacts effectively with group members.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two short sentences or prompts on a small paper slip before leaving.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned to say..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One way I can save energy at home is to..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Opportunities for student and teacher reflection on the lesson and connection to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of speaking about conservation was easy for me today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new way I can help my family conserve energy or water?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The group discussions allowed most students to practice expressing preferences and giving simple suggestions. Some students struggled with consistently using comparatives like "easier than" in fluent sentences; more structured sentence frames might be needed in future speaking tasks. The pronunciation practice for /sh/ and /t/ was beneficial. Overall, the activities effectively promoted interactive speaking, aligning with the lesson's objective.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition):</b> In the next lesson, students will use the vocabulary and grammatical structures (imperatives, comparatives, superlatives) practiced in this speaking lesson to write short paragraphs about their favorite ways to conserve energy at home, creating a "Conservation Tips" poster.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>